WEBVTT

NOTE
Fictional discovery call used as an evaluation fixture. All people and companies are invented.

1
00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:14.923
Calla Whitford: Hi all, thanks for making time. I am Calla from Vantage Forge, and we are meeting Loomwright Home, a direct-to-consumer homeware brand. Maybe we start with who is on the call.

2
00:00:16.123 --> 00:00:27.277
Beatrix Oyelaran: I am the head of e-commerce at Loomwright. We sell rugs, throws and cushions online, mostly through our own site, and we have about eighteen thousand orders a month.

3
00:00:28.477 --> 00:00:33.862
Jonas Kettleby: I am the customer experience manager. I run a team of nine support agents.

4
00:00:35.062 --> 00:00:45.831
Anselm Rhodes: And I am the lead developer. Our storefront is a hosted platform with a few custom apps, and we have a data warehouse that the marketing team uses.

5
00:00:47.031 --> 00:00:50.108
Calla Whitford: Thanks. Beatrix, what are we here to solve?

6
00:00:51.308 --> 00:01:12.077
Beatrix Oyelaran: Two things really, and they are related. Our conversion rate has been flat at one point eight percent for a year, and our support team is drowning in questions that the product pages should answer. Is this rug washable, will this colour match my sofa, what size fits a four by six metre room.

7
00:01:13.277 --> 00:01:22.508
Jonas Kettleby: About sixty percent of our tickets are pre-purchase questions or order status. My agents spend most of their time on the same twenty questions.

8
00:01:23.708 --> 00:01:31.015
Calla Whitford: So a shopping assistant on the site, one that answers product questions and helps customers choose, would address both?

9
00:01:32.215 --> 00:01:49.138
Beatrix Oyelaran: Yes. A conversational assistant that knows our catalogue, and that can recommend items. The goal is to lift conversion by at least ten percent relative, so from one point eight to two percent, and to deflect at least forty percent of the pre-purchase tickets.

10
00:01:50.338 --> 00:02:06.492
Anselm Rhodes: I want to flag that it must not make things up. If a customer asks if a rug is safe for pets and our data does not say, it should say it does not know and offer to connect to a person.

11
00:02:07.692 --> 00:02:19.231
Calla Whitford: Agreed. We would ground every answer in the product data, the care instructions, and your policy pages, and show where the answer came from. If nothing matches, it hands off.

12
00:02:20.431 --> 00:02:28.892
Jonas Kettleby: Handoff to a human agent is a must-have for me. With the conversation history attached, so the customer does not repeat themselves.

13
00:02:30.092 --> 00:02:36.631
Calla Whitford: So the first must-have is a grounded product assistant with a handoff to a human. What else?

14
00:02:37.831 --> 00:02:50.908
Beatrix Oyelaran: Second, personalised recommendations. A customer looks at a sage green throw, and the assistant suggests cushions and a matching rug. We have a bundle strategy, and today the bundles are hand-picked by a merchandiser.

15
00:02:52.108 --> 00:03:00.185
Anselm Rhodes: The catalogue is about twelve hundred products, with attributes like colour, material, size, and style. The attributes are not perfectly clean.

16
00:03:01.385 --> 00:03:12.154
Calla Whitford: That is a typical situation. We would include an enrichment step, where a model reads the product descriptions and fills in missing attributes, which your merchandiser then approves.

17
00:03:13.354 --> 00:03:19.892
Beatrix Oyelaran: Yes, that would be valuable by itself. That is a third feature, catalogue attribute enrichment with approval.

18
00:03:21.092 --> 00:03:28.400
Jonas Kettleby: For order status, can the assistant look up an order if the customer gives an order number and email?

19
00:03:29.600 --> 00:03:41.523
Anselm Rhodes: Our platform has an order API. It needs a token, and I would prefer the assistant only gets read access to a narrow set of fields. Status, carrier and tracking link.

20
00:03:42.723 --> 00:03:53.492
Calla Whitford: That fits. For the proof of concept, we would use a mock order service with the same shape, and then connect to the real API in the pilot.

21
00:03:54.692 --> 00:04:07.000
Beatrix Oyelaran: A fourth thing, and this is nice to have rather than must-have, is a dashboard showing what customers ask. Top questions, unanswered questions, which products cause confusion. That would help our merchandising.

22
00:04:08.200 --> 00:04:15.892
Calla Whitford: We would capture each conversation with tags, and show the top themes. I would call it a conversation insights board.

23
00:04:17.092 --> 00:04:22.092
Jonas Kettleby: Yes please. That would also tell me where to improve the help pages.

24
00:04:23.292 --> 00:04:25.600
Calla Whitford: Let us discuss constraints. Languages? Tone?

25
00:04:26.800 --> 00:04:38.723
Beatrix Oyelaran: English only for now, though we sell into Ireland and Australia. The tone should match our brand: warm, plain and a little witty, never pushy. I will send our tone guide.

26
00:04:39.923 --> 00:04:48.769
Anselm Rhodes: Performance matters too. The chat widget must not hurt page speed. Our Lighthouse score is high and the marketing team watches it closely.

27
00:04:49.969 --> 00:05:03.046
Calla Whitford: We would load the widget lazily and keep it under a small script size budget. I will note that as a constraint, with a target of no measurable change in the largest contentful paint.

28
00:05:04.246 --> 00:05:10.015
Beatrix Oyelaran: And privacy. We should not store personal information in chat logs longer than ninety days.

29
00:05:11.215 --> 00:05:16.600
Calla Whitford: Noted. Ninety day retention for chat logs, with personal details masked in the insights.

30
00:05:17.800 --> 00:05:22.031
Calla Whitford: Let us talk about money. Beatrix, what budget do you have?

31
00:05:23.231 --> 00:05:38.615
Beatrix Oyelaran: For the proof of concept and a four week live test on ten percent of traffic, we can spend sixty thousand dollars. After that, if the numbers hold, we would sign a yearly contract, but that is a separate conversation.

32
00:05:39.815 --> 00:05:43.662
Calla Whitford: Does that include the model costs during the live test?

33
00:05:44.862 --> 00:05:47.938
Beatrix Oyelaran: Yes, everything. So please keep running costs low.

34
00:05:49.138 --> 00:05:53.754
Anselm Rhodes: I would estimate a model cost per conversation, because finance will ask.

35
00:05:54.954 --> 00:06:04.569
Calla Whitford: We will include a cost table per conversation in the architecture, with cheap models for classification and a stronger one only for the final answer.

36
00:06:05.769 --> 00:06:15.385
Beatrix Oyelaran: Timing. Our peak season starts in October. I would like the live test to start in early September, so we need the prototype in July.

37
00:06:16.585 --> 00:06:25.046
Calla Whitford: So a clickable prototype by the middle of July, and a live test in September. Anselm, what do we need from you?

38
00:06:26.246 --> 00:06:39.708
Anselm Rhodes: I can export the product catalogue as a file within a week. And I can set up a sandbox store for you with test orders. I will do both by the end of next week.

39
00:06:40.908 --> 00:06:46.677
Calla Whitford: Perfect. Action for Anselm, catalogue export and sandbox store by the end of next week.

40
00:06:47.877 --> 00:07:02.108
Jonas Kettleby: I can give you anonymised copies of about five hundred past tickets, with the answer that the agent gave. That would give you a real list of questions. Can I have until the first of next month?

41
00:07:03.308 --> 00:07:07.538
Calla Whitford: Yes, five hundred anonymised tickets by the first. Thank you, Jonas.

42
00:07:08.738 --> 00:07:23.738
Beatrix Oyelaran: I will send the tone guide and the list of our top ten bundles by Friday. And I need to confirm with our finance director that the sixty thousand covers the live test, I will do that this week.

43
00:07:24.938 --> 00:07:30.708
Calla Whitford: Great. And what about success metrics for the live test? Beyond conversion and ticket deflection?

44
00:07:31.908 --> 00:07:41.138
Jonas Kettleby: Customer satisfaction on chats at or above four out of five, and fewer than five percent of conversations handed off for the wrong reason.

45
00:07:42.338 --> 00:08:09.646
Calla Whitford: I will include those in the plan. Let me recap what I have. Sector is e-commerce, direct-to-consumer homeware. Must-haves: grounded product assistant with human handoff, personalised recommendations and bundles, and catalogue attribute enrichment with approval. A fourth, nice to have, is a conversation insights board. Constraints: brand tone, lazy loading for page speed, ninety day log retention, read-only order access. Budget is sixty thousand dollars all in. Prototype by mid July.

46
00:08:10.846 --> 00:08:12.846
Beatrix Oyelaran: Yes, that is spot on.

47
00:08:14.046 --> 00:08:32.508
Calla Whitford: Actions again. Anselm sends the catalogue and sandbox store by the end of next week. Jonas sends the five hundred tickets by the first. Beatrix sends the tone guide, the bundles, and confirms the budget this week. I will send notes and a proposed plan within three days.

48
00:08:33.708 --> 00:08:35.708
Jonas Kettleby: Thanks. Looking forward to it.
